WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:05.450
If you only know Wolverine from the movies
or mainline X-Men comics, you really don't

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:06.460
know him at all.

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:11.610
The character got his start from some extremely
humble beginnings, but Wolverine — aka Logan,

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:17.210
aka James Howlett — has grown to be one
of Marvel Comics' most popular characters...and

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:19.370
a bankable star on the big screen to boot.

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:23.670
But even though he's best known for his swagger,
there are more than a few weird and maybe

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:27.450
even embarrassing events scattered throughout
his cigar-chomping history.

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:29.910
Let's have a look at the untold truth of Wolverine...

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:33.020
Punisher, Spock, and a 12-year-old girl

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:37.210
Once Wolverine became a popular character
in Marvel Comics, he started popping up in

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:39.230
a whole lot of crossover stories.

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:43.780
And that's led to some truly insane match-ups
that sometimes left Logan as the loser.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:45.350
Like, a big-time loser.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:51.210
For starters, In writer Garth Ennis' grim
2001 Punisher series, Logan and the Punisher

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:52.219
faced off.

00:00:52.219 --> 00:00:56.640
Being a master tactician, the Punisher went
to extremes to make sure Wolverine couldn't

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.010
come after him for a while.

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:03.290
The story finds the duo in an uneasy alliance
to take out some criminals, but takes a dark

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:07.630
turn when the Punisher decides to just get
rid of Wolverine, too, and unloads a clip

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:09.249
into Wolverine's manhood.

00:01:09.249 --> 00:01:12.030
Healing factor or not, that's gotta hurt.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:14.759
Then he runs him over with a steamroller.

00:01:14.759 --> 00:01:15.819
That's...thorough.

00:01:15.819 --> 00:01:22.049
Wolverine also met the crew of the USS Enterprise
in the 1996 Star Trek/X-Men crossover comic.

00:01:22.049 --> 00:01:26.640
When the X-Men meet Spock, Wolverine doesn't
want to cooperate, and lunges at the Vulcan

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:27.700
to knock him out.

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:32.090
But, since he's a fictional character who's
never seen an episode of Star Trek, Wolverine

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:34.840
is not aware of the infamous Vulcan Nerve
Pinch.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:42.200
It goes something like this:

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:45.439
Spock takes Wolverine down, at least temporarily.

00:01:45.439 --> 00:01:50.749
Later on, Wolverine does get the drop on Spock
after he's knocked down, but Round 1 definitely

00:01:50.749 --> 00:01:52.189
went to Spock.

00:01:52.189 --> 00:01:57.399
Finally, Wolverine also had a small but memorable
role in Runaways, a comic that focused on

00:01:57.399 --> 00:02:01.560
a team of teenagers who realize their parents
are actually super villains.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:06.119
One of those missions brought the team into
the path of a few of Earth’s Mightiest Heroes

00:02:06.119 --> 00:02:08.380
and it did not go well for Wolverine.

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:09.380
At all.

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:14.061
The Runaways' youngest member, 12-year-old
Molly Hayes, is also a mutant.

00:02:14.061 --> 00:02:16.450
Her power is a whole lot of super-strength.

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:20.870
When Molly gets scared that the Avengers want
to take her away from her friends, her fight-or-flight

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:26.090
instincts kicks in — and she punches Wolverine
clean out of the building, with Logan crashing

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:28.840
face first into the snowy street outside.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:31.360
Does that healing factor apply to pride, too?

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:33.560
It’s not Wolf-erine

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:38.360
When Jackman landed the role of Wolverine
in the first X-Men movie back in 2000, he

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:40.340
was woefully unprepared for the role.

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:41.390
How bad was it?

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:46.180
He didn't even realize a wolverine was a real
animal, so he started studying up on wolves

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:48.940
to bring an animalistic quality to his performance.

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:54.120
Jackman wasn't very familiar with the comic
character, so he just assumed Wolverine was

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:57.660
a guy who acted like, well, a big, angry animal.

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:02.620
Yeah, kind of like that.

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:07.100
So he just acted like a wolf in his first
few scenes, to the point that director Bryan

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:09.480
Singer had to ask him to cut it out.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:12.770
He told Rolling Stone about the embarrassing
ordeal in 2013:

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:17.150
"I said, 'Yeah, man, I've been studying wolves
and I think if we could bring that to the

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:18.960
screen—' and he goes, 'What?

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:21.460
You're not a wolf, man, you're a wolverine.'"

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:26.200
Lucky for everyone, Jackman killed it anyway
— animal confusion or not.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:27.200
He was a bad guy

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:32.100
Going back to his first appearance in the
comics, Wolverine was actually introduced

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:38.610
as a bad guy in 1974’s Incredible Hulk #180,
with his first full appearance coming in the

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:39.610
next issue.

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:44.400
The creative team was looking for a new character
to slug it out with the Hulk, since he usually

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:48.730
went through villains pretty quickly — being,
y'know, the Hulk and all.

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:51.560
Wolverine was just the Hulk's latest challenger
at the time.

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:55.790
He wasn’t even introduced in an X-Men story,
and was never actually meant to be a member

00:03:55.790 --> 00:03:56.790
of the team.

00:03:56.790 --> 00:04:00.700
The X-Men were already well-established at
this point, but when the character proved

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:04.881
popular from his time in the Incredible Hulk,
Marvel decided to bring him back for some

00:04:04.881 --> 00:04:09.940
new appearances and flesh him out — eventually
incorporating him into the X-Men as part of

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:12.959
a larger relaunch with several new characters.

00:04:12.959 --> 00:04:18.880
Wolverine next popped up on 1975's Giant-Size
X-Men #1, and the rest is history.

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:24.520
Artist Herb Trimpe, who was the first to draw
Wolverine for publication, told Film Journal

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:29.460
that Wolverine was introduced as a "secondary
or tertiary" character "with no particular

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:31.039
notion of it going anywhere."

00:04:31.039 --> 00:04:32.919
Now he's a flagship character.

00:04:32.919 --> 00:04:36.900
Not bad for a guy who wasn't supposed to live
for more than two issues, right?

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:37.939
Heavy bones

00:04:37.939 --> 00:04:43.319
Wolverine was born with claws made of bone
and a near-limitless ability to heal himself,

00:04:43.319 --> 00:04:45.770
but he wasn't born with that nifty metal skeleton.

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:50.560
That came due to Logan's time in the Weapon
X program, where the shady government group

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:54.770
took advantage of his healing factor to coat
his bones with the super-strong comic book

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:56.310
metal adamantium.

00:04:56.310 --> 00:05:01.020
Marvel says all that shiny bone-coating weighs
a total of 105 pounds.

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:06.590
For the sake of comparison, the average, non-metallic
bone skeleton weighs a bit less than 30 pounds.

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:10.659
So, that’s a whole lot of extra weight to
lug around, even if you are super-jacked,

00:05:10.659 --> 00:05:11.729
man.

00:05:11.729 --> 00:05:13.460
Tiny tough guy

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:17.779
Since he's loosely inspired by the real-life
animal, it's no surprise that Wolverine is

00:05:17.779 --> 00:05:19.310
actually pretty short.

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:24.490
According to Marvel, the comic version of
the character is a mere 5 feet and 3 inches

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:25.490
tall.

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:28.689
In the comics, they do a pretty good job of
showing his small stature when he's hanging

00:05:28.689 --> 00:05:32.460
around in a group, usually making him the
shortest guy in the room.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:37.809
But while the comic character is short, Hugh
Jackman is actually a towering six-foot-two.

00:05:37.809 --> 00:05:42.879
To make sure we wasn't looking down on everyone
else, director Bryan Singer used some classic

00:05:42.879 --> 00:05:47.639
Hollywood angles to at least try and make
the tall leading man look a bit on the shorter

00:05:47.639 --> 00:05:48.639
side.

00:05:48.639 --> 00:05:49.639
Never a manimal

00:05:49.639 --> 00:05:53.400
There's been a rumor going around for decades
that the character of Wolverine was originally

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:59.110
conceived as an actual wolverine — who got
mutated into a man, sorta like the Ninja Turtles,

00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:00.110
but hairier.

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:04.259
In fact, that alleged origin was actually
mentioned in the the DVD extras for X-Men

00:06:04.259 --> 00:06:05.610
Origins: Wolverine.

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:07.569
But it turns out it's not true.

00:06:07.569 --> 00:06:12.330
After hearing this "true" story touted for
years, creator Len Wein broke his silence

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:17.050
to officially deny the reports and tell the
real story of Wolverine's creation.

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:18.050
Here's how he explained it:

00:06:18.050 --> 00:06:21.740
"I absolutely did not ever intend to make
Logan a mutated wolverine.

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:27.439
I write stories about human beings, not evolved
animals … The mutated wolverine thing came

00:06:27.439 --> 00:06:30.789
about long after I was no longer involved
with the book."

00:06:30.789 --> 00:06:33.860
And what does Wolverine himself think of the
rumor?

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:37.040
Movie mainstay

00:06:37.059 --> 00:06:41.590
Where some comic franchises have already been
rebooted a few times, X-Men is still going

00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:46.080
strong in roughly the same continuity launched
all the way back in 2000.

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:51.229
Okay, sure, X-Men: Days of Future Past did
some housecleaning with the timeline, but

00:06:51.229 --> 00:06:54.199
it's still a lot of the same actors all these
years later.

00:06:54.199 --> 00:06:58.499
But even when the series was prequel-ized
with younger actors as part of First Class,

00:06:58.499 --> 00:07:00.759
the ageless Wolverine was still around.

00:07:00.759 --> 00:07:06.139
Hugh Jackman starred in the first three X-Men
films, plus X-Men Origins: Wolverine, then

00:07:06.139 --> 00:07:11.509
in the similarly titled sequel, The Wolverine,
and closed out his solo film series in the

00:07:11.509 --> 00:07:12.589
beloved Logan.

00:07:12.589 --> 00:07:17.780
He also played a key role in X-Men: Days of
Future Past, and had bit roles in X-Men: First

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:19.839
Class and X-Men: Apocalypse.

00:07:19.839 --> 00:07:21.240
The only film he wasn't in?

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:23.070
The R-rated hit Deadpool.

00:07:23.070 --> 00:07:27.439
But even though Wolverine didn't show up in
the flesh, Ryan Reynolds' foul-mouthed mercenary

00:07:27.439 --> 00:07:31.340
did sport a Jackman mask and make plenty of
Wolverine jokes.

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:34.719
So, really, he was there in spirit — and
that's good enough for us.

00:07:34.719 --> 00:07:38.729
"Who's b----- did I have to fondle to get
my very own movie?

00:07:38.729 --> 00:07:41.629
I can't tell you, but it does rhyme with 'Pullverine.'"

00:07:41.629 --> 00:07:43.699
Thanks for watching!

00:07:43.699 --> 00:07:46.819
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:46.819 --> 00:07:48.909
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love too!

